### 3\. Safe Ettercap usage commands

Assume your interface is `eth0`. Replace it with the interface shown by `ip -br addr`.

A. Start text-mode inspection

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0
```

B. Observe only your own host's traffic

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -p
```

C. Read a previously recorded PCAP file

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -r capture.pcap
```

D. Save a packet capture

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -w capture.pcap
```

E. Inspect TCP traffic

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -t tcp
```

F. Inspect UDP traffic

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -t udp
```

G. Inspect all supported traffic

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -t all
```

H. List available plugins

```bash
ettercap -P list
```

I. Inspect Ettercap logs

```bash
etterlog -h
man etterlog
```

Important correction: `-t` selects TCP/UDP protocol types; it does not select ARP. For more specific packet filtering, use `-f` with a valid capture filter.

### 4\. Ettercap target syntax

Ettercap supports target expressions involving MAC addresses, IPv4 addresses, IPv6 addresses and ports.

General structure:

```text
MAC/IP/IPv6/PORTS
```

For example, a target expression can identify a particular IP address or port range.

Do not confuse target selection with a packet-capture filter: they serve different purposes.

### 5\. Ettercap plugin investigation

Run:

```bash
ettercap -P list
```

Then investigate a particular plugin:

```bash
man ettercap_plugins
```

Plugin availability varies with the installed build.

For your research, concentrate on understanding what each plugin observes, what it changes and what evidence it produces. Avoid activating interception, credential collection or traffic-modification plugins against other people's devices.

