Bettercap has a different architecture. It uses modules, parameters, commands and caplets.

### 1\. Installation and verification

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install bettercap -y
```

Check installation:

```bash
bettercap -version
bettercap -h
which bettercap
```

Start Bettercap:

```bash
sudo bettercap
```

Start with a particular interface:

```bash
sudo bettercap -iface eth0
```

### 2\. Bettercap core commands

These commands are entered inside the Bettercap console.

| Command        | Purpose                          |
| -------------- | -------------------------------- |
| help           | Show available commands          |
| help MODULE    | Show module-specific help        |
| active         | Show active modules              |
| get            | Display parameters               |
| get *          | Display all available parameters |
| get NAME       | Read a parameter                 |
| set NAME VALUE | Change a parameter               |
| clear          | Clear console                    |
| sleep 10       | Wait 10 seconds                  |
| quit           | Exit Bettercap                   |
| q              | Exit Bettercap                   |
| include FILE   | Load another caplet              |
| !COMMAND       | Execute a shell command          |
| alias MAC NAME | Assign an endpoint alias         |

bettercap +1

### 3\. Network reconnaissance module

Safe discovery in an authorized lab

Start:

```text
net.recon on
```

Display discovered hosts:

```text
net.show
```

Show information about a particular address:

```text
net.show 192.168.1.10
```

Show endpoint metadata:

```text
net.show.meta 192.168.1.10
```

Stop discovery:

```text
net.recon off
```

Clear discovered endpoints:

```text
net.clear
```

The `net.recon` module periodically reads the operating system's ARP table to discover network devices. bettercap

### 4\. Network probing module

The `net.probe` module sends discovery probes to IP addresses on the current subnet.

Start:

```text
net.probe on
```

Stop:

```text
net.probe off
```

Inspect its configuration:

```text
help net.probe
```

Read its parameters:

```text
get net.probe
```

Its discovery mechanisms include mDNS, NBNS, UPnP and WSD. bettercap +1

### 5\. Event monitoring

Start event monitoring:

```text
events.stream on
```

Stop:

```text
events.stream off
```

Events can help you understand which activities Bettercap is reporting.

### 6\. Module discovery

One of the most important Bettercap commands is:

```text
help
```

It displays the modules available in your installed build.

For individual module documentation:

```text
help net.recon
help net.probe
```

Use this to discover the remaining modules instead of assuming every installation supports exactly the same commands.

### 7\. Bettercap command-line options

| Option        | Purpose                     |
| ------------- | --------------------------- |
| -h            | Help                        |
| -version      | Version information         |
| -iface        | Select network interface    |
| -caplet       | Load a caplet               |
| -caplets-path | Specify caplet directory    |
| -eval         | Execute console commands    |
| -script       | Load a session script       |
| -autostart    | Automatically start modules |
| -debug        | Enable debugging            |
| -silent       | Suppress non-error logs     |

Examples:

Start an interactive session:

```bash
sudo bettercap -iface eth0
```

Run a discovery session:

```bash
sudo bettercap -iface eth0 \
-eval "net.recon on; net.show"
```

Display available caplets:

```bash
find /usr/share/bettercap -type f -name '*.cap'
```

Load a caplet:

```bash
sudo bettercap -caplet /path/to/lab.cap
```

The caplet must exist and contain valid commands.

